1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,837
Chapter 54: Pulmonary Disorders.

3
00:00:05,837 --> 00:00:17,287
Asthma Acute Bronchitis Pneumonia Tuberculosis Sarcoidosis Cystic Fibrosis Carbon Monoxide Poisoning References During pregnancy, pulmonary disorders are frequently encountered.

4
00:00:17,287 --> 00:00:26,400
In one study, asthma and community-acquired pneumonia accounted for almost 10 percent of nonobstetrical antepartum hospitalizations .

5
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:36,587
Pregnant women, especially those in the last trimester, tolerate lung disease poorly because of important pregnancy-induced changes in ventilatory physiology .

6
00:00:36,587 --> 00:00:40,662
Lung volumes and capacities are significantly altered.

7
00:00:40,662 --> 00:00:46,000
In turn, these shift gas concentrations and acid-base values in blood.

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:57,375
These important and sometimes marked changes are reviewed in Chapter 4 (p. 66), and normal values for blood gas can be found in the Appendix (p. 1232).

9
00:00:57,375 --> 00:01:07,212
Some of these can be summarized as follows: 1. Vital capacity and inspiratory capacity increase by approximately 20 percent by late pregnancy.

10
00:01:07,212 --> 00:01:15,462
2. Expiratory reserve volume declines from 1300 mL to approximately 1100 mL.

11
00:01:15,462 --> 00:01:22,350
3. Progesterone-driven respiratory stimulation raises tidal volume approximately 40 percent.

12
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:28,462
4. Minute ventilation increases 30 to 40 percent due to higher tidal volume.

13
00:01:28,462 --> 00:01:37,687
As a result, arterial partial pressure of oxygen (PaO2) rises from 100 to 105 mm Hg. 5.

14
00:01:37,687 --> 00:01:45,050
Expanded metabolic demands cause a 30-percent increase in carbon dioxide (CO2) production.

15
00:01:45,050 --> 00:01:55,325
But, because of its concomitantly greater diffusion capacity and hyperventilation, the PaCO2 is reduced from 40 to 32 mm Hg. 6.

16
00:01:55,325 --> 00:02:03,187
Residual volume diminishes approximately 20 percent from 1500 mL to 1200 mL.

17
00:02:03,187 --> 00:02:10,237
7. An expanding uterus and higher intraabdominal pressure lower chest wall compliance by one third.

18
00:02:10,237 --> 00:02:19,350
This causes a 10- to 25-percent reduction in functional residual capacity—the sum of expiratory reserve and residual volumes.

19
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:27,287
Beginning at 14 to 16 weeks’ gestation forced vital capacity and peak expiratory flow progressively increase .

20
00:02:27,287 --> 00:02:34,775
The result of these pregnancy-induced changes is substantively greater ventilation due to deeper but not more frequent breathing.

21
00:02:34,775 --> 00:02:44,637
This is thought to be stimulated by basal oxygen consumption, which incrementally rises from 20 to 40 mL/min in the second half of pregnancy.

22
00:02:44,637 --> 00:02:46,125
Asthma.

23
00:02:46,125 --> 00:02:48,925
# Pathophysiology.

24
00:02:48,925 --> 00:02:54,537
Asthma is a chronic inflammatory airway syndrome with a major hereditary component.

25
00:02:54,537 --> 00:03:11,075
Polymorphism genes on chromosome 5q that include cytokine gene clusters, β-adrenergic and glucocorticoid receptor genes, and the T-cell antigen receptor gene are associated with greater airway responsiveness and persistent subacute inflammation .

26
00:03:11,075 --> 00:03:18,025
Racial differences are seen, and morbidity rates are disproportionately higher in black compared with white women .

27
00:03:18,025 --> 00:03:29,550
Asthma is etiologically and clinically heterogeneous, and an environmental allergic stimulant such as influenza or cigarette smoke serves as a promoter for susceptible individuals.

28
00:03:29,550 --> 00:03:39,675
The hallmarks of asthma are reversible airway obstruction from bronchial smooth muscle contraction, vascular congestion, tenacious mucus, and mucosal edema.

29
00:03:39,675 --> 00:03:46,225
Stimuli cause acute infiltration of eosinophils, mast cells, and T lymphocytes.

30
00:03:46,225 --> 00:03:57,650
Inflammatory mediators produced by these and other cells include histamine, leukotrienes, prostaglandins, cytokines, immunoglobulin E (IgE), and many others.

31
00:03:57,650 --> 00:04:05,900
F-series prostaglandins and ergonovine exacerbate asthma, and these commonly used uterotonics are avoided if possible.

32
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:08,375
# Clinical Course.

33
00:04:08,375 --> 00:04:16,312
Asthma manifestations range from mild wheezing to severe bronchoconstriction, which obstructs airways and decreases airflow.

34
00:04:16,312 --> 00:04:28,075
This lowers the forced expiratory volume in 1 second/forced vital capacity (FEV1/FVC) ratio and the peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR).

35
00:04:28,075 --> 00:04:35,012
The work of breathing progressively increases, and patients note chest tightness, wheezing, or breathlessness.

36
00:04:35,012 --> 00:04:41,387
Subsequent worsening of oxygenation primarily reflects ventilation–perfusion mismatching.

37
00:04:41,387 --> 00:04:45,762
This results from an uneven distribution of airway narrowing.

38
00:04:45,762 --> 00:04:54,287
Varied manifestations of asthma have led to a classification system that considers severity, onset, and duration of symptoms .

39
00:04:54,287 --> 00:04:58,537
With persistent or worsening bronchial obstruction, clinical

40
00:04:58,708 --> 00:05:02,870
stages progress as shown in Figure 54-1.

41
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:13,320
Hypoxia initially is mitigated by hyperventilation, which maintains the PaO2 within a normal range but lowers PaCO2, creating respiratory alkalosis.

42
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:22,145
As airway narrowing worsens, the degree of ventilation–perfusion defects is accentuated, and arterial hypoxemia ensues.

43
00:05:22,145 --> 00:05:28,520
With severe obstruction, fatigue causes early CO2 retention and impairs ventilation.

44
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:35,470
Because of hyperventilation, this may only be seen initially as the PaCO2 returning to the normal range.

45
00:05:35,470 --> 00:05:39,808
With continuing obstruction, respiratory failure follows.

46
00:05:39,808 --> 00:05:42,370
Table 54-1.

47
00:05:42,370 --> 00:05:46,770
Classification of Asthma Severity Figure 54-1.

48
00:05:46,770 --> 00:05:49,283
Clinical stages of asthma.

49
00:05:49,283 --> 00:05:54,133
FEV1 = forced expiratory volume in 1 second.

50
00:05:54,133 --> 00:06:00,395
These changes are generally reversible and well tolerated by the healthy nonpregnant individual.

51
00:06:00,395 --> 00:06:06,283
However, even early asthma stages may be dangerous for the pregnant woman and her fetus.

52
00:06:06,283 --> 00:06:15,795
This is because diminished functional residual capacity and greater pulmonary shunting render the gravida more susceptible to hypoxia and hypoxemia.

53
00:06:15,795 --> 00:06:28,658
Effects of Pregnancy on Asthma Chronic asthma or an acute exacerbation affects up to 8 percent of gravidas (Baghlaf, 2019; Flores, 2020; Mazurek, 2018).

54
00:06:28,658 --> 00:06:34,883
Pulmonary function changes in pregnancy are more pronounced in asthmatics compared with healthy women .

55
00:06:34,883 --> 00:06:40,333
Despite this, pregnancy has an unpredictable effect on underlying asthma.

56
00:06:40,333 --> 00:06:48,295
In a review of more than 2000 gravidas, approximately a third each improved, remained unchanged, or clearly worsened .

57
00:06:48,295 --> 00:06:52,070
Exacerbations are more common with severe disease .

58
00:06:52,070 --> 00:07:00,208
In a study by Schatz and associates (2003), baseline severity correlated with asthma morbidity during pregnancy.

59
00:07:00,208 --> 00:07:15,420
With mild disease, 13 percent of women had an exacerbation and 2.3 percent required admission; with moderate disease, these numbers were 26 and 7 percent; and for severe asthma, 52 and 27 percent.

60
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:22,358
Others have reported similar findings (Charlton, 2013; Hendler, 2006).

61
00:07:22,358 --> 00:07:25,995
Intrapartum exacerbation rates are controversial.

62
00:07:25,995 --> 00:07:32,820
In one study, 20 percent of women with mild or moderate asthma had an intrapartum exacerbation .

63
00:07:32,820 --> 00:07:41,958
Mabie and coworkers (1992) reported an 18-fold greater exacerbation risk following cesarean versus vaginal delivery.

64
00:07:41,958 --> 00:07:51,020
Conversely, Wendel and colleagues (1996) reported exacerbations at the time of delivery in only 1 percent of asthmatics.

65
00:07:51,020 --> 00:07:59,183
Pregnancy Outcome Asthma remains a potential risk factor for worse pregnancy outcomes despite continued improvements in treatment.

66
00:07:59,183 --> 00:08:03,870
Data from two large studies are shown in Table 54-2.

67
00:08:03,870 --> 00:08:12,120
The incidences of small-for-gestational-age neonates and perinatal mortality also are significantly greater in women with asthma .

68
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:16,620
However, these findings are not consistent among all studies.

69
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:26,895
A study of approximately 3000 pregnancies complicated by asthma showed higher rates of maternal pneumonia and cesarean delivery but not of perinatal mortality .

70
00:08:26,895 --> 00:08:37,008
In a European report of 37,585 pregnancies of women with asthma, the risks for most obstetrical complications were not increased .

71
00:08:37,008 --> 00:08:45,495
Blais and associates (2013) noted that the incidence of spontaneous abortion may be slightly greater in women with asthma.

72
00:08:45,495 --> 00:08:48,070
Table 54-2.

73
00:08:48,070 --> 00:08:57,845
Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes in Women with Asthma Some evidence suggests that severe disease, poor control, or both is linked to higher morbidity rates.

74
00:08:57,845 --> 00:09:10,033
In a study by the Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units (MFMU) Network, delivery before 37 weeks’ gestation was not more frequent in pregnancies of women with asthma compared with controls .

75
00:09:10,033 --> 00:09:15,758
However, for women with severe asthma, the rate was approximately twofold higher.

76
00:09:15,758 --> 00:09:28,395
The MFMU Network study suggests also a direct relationship of baseline pregnancy FEV1 with birthweight and an inverse relationship with rates of gestational hypertension and preterm delivery .

77
00:09:28,395 --> 00:09:38,520
Kemppainen and coworkers (2018) found that women requiring treatment for asthma during pregnancy are at greater risk for adverse perinatal outcomes.

78
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:53,433
Maternal morbidity rises markedly with status asthmaticus and includes life-threatening complications such as muscle fatigue with respiratory arrest, pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, acute cor pulmonale, and cardiac arrhythmias.

79
00:09:53,596 --> 00:10:01,296
Not surprisingly, maternal and perinatal mortality rates rise substantively when mechanical ventilation is required.

80
00:10:01,246 --> 00:10:13,033
Fetal Effects Animal and human studies suggest that development of maternal respiratory alkalosis leads to fetal hypoxemia well before the alkalosis compromises maternal oxygenation .

81
00:10:13,033 --> 00:10:25,483
Theories hypothesize that the fetus is jeopardized by decreased uterine blood flow, reduced maternal venous return, and an alkaline-induced leftward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve .

82
00:10:25,483 --> 00:10:35,933
The fetal response to maternal hypoxemia is lower umbilical blood flow, higher systemic and pulmonary vascular resistance, and decreased cardiac output.

83
00:10:35,933 --> 00:10:42,146
Several studies cited above confirm that fetal-growth restriction rates rise with asthma severity.

84
00:10:42,146 --> 00:10:47,833
Because the fetus may be seriously compromised, aggressive management is necessary.

85
00:10:47,833 --> 00:10:53,583
Monitoring the fetal response is, in effect, an indicator of maternal status.

86
00:10:53,583 --> 00:11:01,833
A return of moderate variability or accelerations in the fetal heart rate is an indicator of improving maternal oxygen status.

87
00:11:01,833 --> 00:11:08,096
Possible teratogenic or adverse fetal effects of drugs given to control asthma are a concern.

88
00:11:08,096 --> 00:11:26,571
Several reports show a slightly greater risk for abnormalities such as cleft lip and palate and autism spectrum disorders, but further verification is necessary (Eltonsy, 2016; Gidaya, 2016; Murphy, 2013b; Wang, 2014).

89
00:11:26,571 --> 00:11:33,671
It is worrisome that up to half of women discontinue essential treatment between 5- and 13-weeks’ gestation .

90
00:11:33,671 --> 00:11:36,508
# Clinical Evaluation.

91
00:11:36,508 --> 00:11:43,758
The subjective severity of asthma frequently does not correlate with objective measures of airway function or ventilation.

92
00:11:43,758 --> 00:11:56,346
Clinical examination also can be an inaccurate predictor, but useful signs include labored breathing, tachycardia, pulsus paradoxus, prolonged expiration, and use of accessory muscles.

93
00:11:56,346 --> 00:12:02,796
Signs of a potentially fatal exacerbation include central cyanosis and altered consciousness.

94
00:12:02,796 --> 00:12:07,383
Pulmonary function testing should be routine in the management of asthma.

95
00:12:07,383 --> 00:12:13,433
Sequential measurement of the FEV1 or of the PEFR is the best measure of severity .

96
00:12:13,433 --> 00:12:25,096
An FEV1 <1 L, or <20 percent of predicted value, correlates with severe disease defined by hypoxia, poor response to therapy, and a high relapse rate.

97
00:12:25,096 --> 00:12:32,871
The PEFR correlates well with the FEV1, and it can be measured reliably with inexpensive portable meters.

98
00:12:32,871 --> 00:12:40,458
It is advantageous for each woman to determine her own baseline when asymptomatic to compare with values when symptomatic.

99
00:12:40,458 --> 00:12:43,596
# Management of Chronic Asthma.

100
00:12:43,596 --> 00:12:52,246
Asthma management by an experienced team produces better outcomes (Lim, 2014; Wendel, 1996).

101
00:12:52,246 --> 00:13:03,446
Management guidelines include: 1. Patient education about general asthma management and its effect on pregnancy (Bonham, 2018; Robijn, 2019).

102
00:13:03,446 --> 00:13:08,371
2. Avoidance or control of precipitating environmental factors.

103
00:13:08,371 --> 00:13:16,783
Viral infections that include the common cold are frequent triggers (Ali, 2013; Murphy, 2013a).

104
00:13:16,783 --> 00:13:20,871
Influenza and pneumococcal vaccination is encouraged.

105
00:13:20,871 --> 00:13:31,908
3. Objective assessment of pulmonary function with PEFR or FEV1 and of fetal status with serial growth ultrasound and antepartum testing if indicated .

106
00:13:31,908 --> 00:13:40,133
4. Pharmacological therapy with appropriate combinations and doses to provide baseline control and treat exacerbations.

107
00:13:40,133 --> 00:13:44,808
Periodic medication reviews are helpful to avoid noncompliance .

108
00:13:44,808 --> 00:13:52,858
5. Treatment of related disorders including smoking, depression, rhinitis, and gastroesophageal reflux .

109
00:13:52,858 --> 00:14:01,508
In general, women with moderate to severe asthma ideally measure and record either their FEV1 or PEFR twice daily.

110
00:14:01,508 --> 00:14:06,146
The FEV1 ideally is >80 percent of predicted.

111
00:14:06,146 --> 00:14:13,033
For PEFR, predicted values range from 380 to 550 L/min.

112
00:14:13,033 --> 00:14:19,208
Each woman has her own baseline value, and therapeutic adjustments can be made using this .

113
00:14:19,208 --> 00:14:29,208
Another described method is a management algorithm using fractional exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO) as a noninvasive marker of airway inflammation .

114
00:14:29,208 --> 00:14:33,958
Asthma exacerbations were reduced by 50 percent with this approach .

115
00:14:33,958 --> 00:14:38,446
Agents for Chronic Treatment Selection depends on disease severity.

116
00:14:38,446 --> 00:14:44,333
No therapeutic regimen for management of asthma in pregnancy is universally accepted .

117
00:14:44,333 --> 00:14:48,721
βagonists help abate bronchospasm, and corticosteroids

118
00:14:48,892 --> 00:14:50,942
treat inflammation.

119
00:14:50,892 --> 00:14:57,329
Stepwise regimens recommended for outpatient management are listed in Figure 54-2.

120
00:14:57,329 --> 00:15:02,117
Figure 54-2: Stepwise approach to asthma treatment.

121
00:15:02,117 --> 00:15:10,717
ICS = inhaled corticosteroids; LABA = longacting β-agonists; OCS = oral corticosteroids.

122
00:15:10,717 --> 00:15:17,279
Barnes, 2018; National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute, 2007.

123
00:15:17,279 --> 00:15:23,929
For mild intermittent asthma, short-acting inhaled β-agonists as needed are usually sufficient.

124
00:15:23,929 --> 00:15:27,792
Table 54-3 lists suitable agents.

125
00:15:27,792 --> 00:15:30,417
Table 54-3.

126
00:15:30,417 --> 00:15:38,279
Chronic Asthma Treatment For persistent asthma, most inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are administered twice daily.

127
00:15:38,279 --> 00:15:42,804
The goal is to reduce β-agonists use for symptomatic relief.

128
00:15:42,804 --> 00:15:51,979
Initial low-dose ICS may be augmented to mediumranging dosing if the moderate or severe symptoms shown in Table 54-1 persist.

129
00:15:51,979 --> 00:16:02,692
A case-control study from Canada with a cohort of more than 15,600 nonpregnant women with asthma showed that ICS reduced hospitalizations by 80 percent .

130
00:16:02,692 --> 00:16:13,942
At Parkland Hospital, Wendel and colleagues (1996) achieved a 55-percent reduction in readmissions when severe exacerbations were treated with ICS.

131
00:16:13,942 --> 00:16:27,792
In one study of nonpregnant subjects with mild asthma, as-needed budesonide-formoterol, which is an aerosol ICS and β-agonist combination, was superior to albuterol alone to prevent exacerbations .

132
00:16:27,792 --> 00:16:30,892
This is incorporated in new guidelines .

133
00:16:30,892 --> 00:16:35,717
Theophylline has been used less frequently since ICS became available.

134
00:16:35,717 --> 00:16:41,304
Minimal benefit is gained with these compounds, and they have a high rate of side effects.

135
00:16:41,304 --> 00:16:51,154
However, some theophylline derivatives are considered useful for oral maintenance therapy if the initial response to ICS and β-agonists is not optimal .

136
00:16:51,154 --> 00:16:59,454
Of other agents, antileukotrienes inhibit leukotriene synthesis and include zileuton, zafirlukast, and montelukast.

137
00:16:59,454 --> 00:17:06,229
These drugs are given orally or by inhalation for prevention, but they are ineffective for acute disease .

138
00:17:06,229 --> 00:17:12,517
For maintenance, they are used in conjunction with ICS to allow minimal corticosteroid dosing.

139
00:17:12,517 --> 00:17:17,392
Approximately half of individuals with asthma will improve with these drugs.

140
00:17:17,392 --> 00:17:23,954
However, they are less effective than ICS, and experience with their use in pregnancy is limited .

141
00:17:23,954 --> 00:17:30,904
Last, cromones, which are mast cell degranulation inhibitors, are ineffective for acute asthma.

142
00:17:30,904 --> 00:17:34,604
They are used primarily to treat childhood asthma.

143
00:17:34,604 --> 00:17:42,242
Vitamin D Epidemiological data support an association between vitamin D insufficiency and asthma severity.

144
00:17:42,242 --> 00:17:49,042
The proposed mechanism is modulation of immunological pathways that diminish inflammatory responses .

145
00:17:49,042 --> 00:17:56,517
Jensen and coworkers (2019) reported that pregnant women with asthma have low vitamin D levels.

146
00:17:56,517 --> 00:18:03,367
One systematic review suggested that increasing maternal vitamin D intake might prevent childhood asthma .

147
00:18:03,367 --> 00:18:11,804
Adams and colleagues (2021) reported an association between decreased maternal vitamin D levels and childhood wheezing.

148
00:18:11,804 --> 00:18:20,729
However, a randomized trial with antenatal vitamin D supplementation showed no lowering of childhood asthma risks at 6 years of age .

149
00:18:20,729 --> 00:18:23,829
# Management of Acute Asthma.

150
00:18:23,829 --> 00:18:31,967
Early Treatment Treatment of acute asthma during pregnancy is similar to that for the nonpregnant patient (Figure 54-3).

151
00:18:31,967 --> 00:18:36,992
Importantly, the threshold for hospitalization is significantly lower.

152
00:18:36,992 --> 00:18:45,017
Intravenous (IV) hydration may help clear pulmonary secretions, and supplemental oxygen is given by mask.

153
00:18:45,017 --> 00:18:56,029
The therapeutic aim is to maintain the PaO2 >60 mm Hg, and preferably normal, along with 90- to 95-percent oxygen saturation.

154
00:18:56,029 --> 00:19:01,667
Baseline pulmonary function testing includes FEV1 or PEFR.

155
00:19:01,667 --> 00:19:10,079
Continuous pulse oximetry and electronic fetal monitoring, depending on gestational age, may provide useful information.

156
00:19:10,079 --> 00:19:23,217
Arterial blood gas analysis aides in assessing the severity of an acute attack by providing objective assessment of maternal oxygenation, ventilation, and acid-base status (see Fig. 54-1).

157
00:19:23,217 --> 00:19:34,892
However, Wendel and associates (1996) found that routine arterial blood gas analysis did not help to manage most pregnant women who required admission for asthma control.

158
00:19:34,892 --> 00:19:40,754
If used, the results must be interpreted in relation to normal values for pregnancy.

159
00:19:40,754 --> 00:19:50,267
For example, a PaCO2 >35 mm Hg with a pH <7.35 is consistent with hyperventilation

160
00:19:50,428 --> 00:19:53,815
and CO2 retention in a pregnant woman.

161
00:19:53,765 --> 00:19:59,753
Antibiotics are not given unless pneumonitis is comorbid (p. 962) .

162
00:19:59,753 --> 00:20:02,403
Figure 54-3.

163
00:20:02,403 --> 00:20:06,790
Protocol for management of acute asthma at Parkland Hospital.

164
00:20:06,790 --> 00:20:09,103
aOf predicted value.

165
00:20:09,103 --> 00:20:14,290
bLabor & delivery unit, recovery area, or intensive care unit.

166
00:20:14,290 --> 00:20:23,715
FEV = forced expiratory volume at 1 1 second; PEFR = peak expiratory flow rate (liters per minute).

167
00:20:23,715 --> 00:20:40,053
First-line therapy for acute asthma includes a short-acting β-adrenergic agonist, such as terbutaline, albuterol, isoetharine, epinephrine, isoproterenol, or metaproterenol, which are given subcutaneously, taken orally, or inhaled.

168
00:20:40,053 --> 00:20:44,715
In severely ill women, these drugs can be given intravenously .

169
00:20:44,715 --> 00:20:50,403
Systemic corticosteroids are given early to all patients with severe acute asthma.

170
00:20:50,403 --> 00:21:00,765
One regimen is oral prednisone or prednisolone or IV methylprednisolone in a dose of 30 to 45 mg daily for 5 to 10 days without tapering .

171
00:21:00,765 --> 00:21:06,940
At Parkland Hospital, we use a higher daily IV dose (see Figure 54-3).

172
00:21:06,940 --> 00:21:15,528
Because their onset of action is several hours, corticosteroids are given initially along with β-agonists for severe acute asthma.

173
00:21:15,528 --> 00:21:21,665
If the response is unsatisfactory, a nebulized anticholinergic drug may be added.

174
00:21:21,665 --> 00:21:26,103
These drugs act to relax smooth muscle and diminish mucus.

175
00:21:26,103 --> 00:21:35,390
By nebulizer, a 0.5-mg dose of ipratropium bromide is provided every 20 minutes as needed for three consecutive doses.

176
00:21:35,390 --> 00:21:39,415
Additional doses every 6 to 8 hours can be used .

177
00:21:39,415 --> 00:21:46,353
Also, for severe exacerbations, IV magnesium sulfate or theophylline may prove efficacious.

178
00:21:46,353 --> 00:21:50,915
Further management depends on the severity and response to therapy.

179
00:21:50,915 --> 00:22:01,840
If initial therapy with a β-agonist is associated with FEV1 or PEFR improvement to a level >70 percent of baseline, discharge can be considered.

180
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:13,690
For the woman with obvious respiratory distress or with an FEV1 or PEFR <70 percent of predicted after three doses of a β-agonist, admission is usually advisable .

181
00:22:13,690 --> 00:22:26,528
Intensive therapy is continued with short-acting inhaled β-agonists, IV corticosteroids, inhaled anticholinergics, and close observation for worsening respiratory distress or fatigue in breathing .

182
00:22:26,528 --> 00:22:36,503
The woman is cared for in the delivery unit or an intermediate or intensive care unit (ICU) (Dombrowski, 2006; Zeeman, 2003).

183
00:22:36,503 --> 00:22:47,040
Status Asthmaticus and Respiratory Failure Severe asthma of any type not responding after 30 to 60 minutes of intensive therapy is termed status asthmaticus.

184
00:22:47,040 --> 00:22:54,990
Generally, management of nonpregnant patients with status asthmaticus in an intensive care setting results in a good outcome.

185
00:22:54,990 --> 00:23:05,490
In pregnant women, consideration should be given to early intubation when maternal respiratory status worsens despite aggressive treatment (see Fig. 54-1).

186
00:23:05,490 --> 00:23:12,078
Fatigue, CO2 retention, and hypoxemia are indications for mechanical ventilation .

187
00:23:12,078 --> 00:23:26,628
In refractory cases, venovenous extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) has been successfully used for status asthmaticus during pregnancy (Clifford, 2018; Steinack, 2017).

188
00:23:26,628 --> 00:23:29,215
# Labor and Delivery.

189
00:23:29,215 --> 00:23:38,740
For the laboring patient who has symptomatic asthma, the PEFR or FEV1 is determined on admission, and serial measurements are taken.

190
00:23:38,740 --> 00:23:45,540
Maintenance medications are continued through delivery, and stress-dose corticosteroids are not necessary .

191
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:52,078
Oxytocin or prostaglandins E1 or E2 can be used for cervical ripening and induction.

192
00:23:52,078 --> 00:24:00,678
A non-histamine-releasing narcotic such as fentanyl may be preferable to meperidine for labor, and epidural analgesia is ideal.

193
00:24:00,678 --> 00:24:08,678
For surgical delivery, conduction analgesia is preferred because tracheal intubation can trigger severe bronchospasm.

194
00:24:08,678 --> 00:24:17,715
Postpartum hemorrhage is treated with oxytocin or prostaglandin E1 (misoprostol) or E2 (dinoprostone [Prostin]).

195
00:24:17,715 --> 00:24:24,965
Prostaglandin F2α (Hemabate) is contraindicated because it may cause significant bronchospasm.

196
00:24:24,965 --> 00:24:27,153
Acute Bronchitis.

197
00:24:27,153 --> 00:24:31,853
Large airway infection is manifest by cough without pneumonitis.

198
00:24:31,853 --> 00:24:35,703
It is common in adults, especially in winter months.

199
00:24:35,703 --> 00:24:48,990
Infections are usually caused by viruses, and of these, influenza A and B, parainfluenza, respiratory syncytial, coronavirus, adenovirus, and rhinovirus are frequent isolates .

200
00:24:48,990 --> 00:24:53,665
Bacterial agents causing community-acquired pneumonia are rarely

201
00:24:53,836 --> 00:24:55,523
implicated.

202
00:24:55,473 --> 00:25:02,161
The cough of acute bronchitis persists for 10 to 20 days and occasionally lasts for a month or longer.

203
00:25:02,161 --> 00:25:07,036
Evidence supporting the benefits of antimicrobial therapy is limited .

204
00:25:07,036 --> 00:25:15,148
As supportive care, cough suppressants containing dextromethorphan and expectorants with guaifenesin appear safe in pregnancy .

205
00:25:15,148 --> 00:25:16,798
Pneumonia.

206
00:25:16,798 --> 00:25:21,398
Infection of the lungs is a leading cause of death in the United States .

207
00:25:21,398 --> 00:25:30,648
Current classification includes community-acquired pneumonia (CAP), which is typically encountered in healthy young women, including gravidas.

208
00:25:30,648 --> 00:25:38,611
Patients in outpatient care facilities develop an infection that more closely resembles hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP) .

209
00:25:38,611 --> 00:25:43,848
In most cases of CAP, the offending pathogen is not identified.

210
00:25:43,848 --> 00:25:54,948
In a study from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), pathogens were identified in only 38 percent of nearly 2500 adults with pneumonia .

211
00:25:54,948 --> 00:26:04,323
These included viruses in 23 percent, bacteria in 11 percent, both in 3 percent, and fungi or protozoa in 1 percent.

212
00:26:04,323 --> 00:26:11,036
These percentages changed in 2020 due to the COVID-19 pandemic (p. 964).

213
00:26:11,036 --> 00:26:15,261
Half of bacterial isolates were Streptococcus pneumoniae.

214
00:26:15,261 --> 00:26:22,811
Pneumonia in pregnant women is relatively common (Brito, 2011; Sheffield, 2009).

215
00:26:22,811 --> 00:26:33,086
Gazmararian and coworkers (2002) reported that pneumonia accounts for 4.2 percent of antepartum admissions for nonobstetrical complications.

216
00:26:33,086 --> 00:26:37,823
Pneumonia is also a frequent indication for postpartum readmission .

217
00:26:37,823 --> 00:26:44,511
Admission rates for respiratory illnesses during influenza season are higher than rates in the remaining months.

218
00:26:44,511 --> 00:26:50,061
Regardless of etiology, mortality from pneumonia is infrequent in young women.

219
00:26:50,061 --> 00:27:01,961
However, during pregnancy, severe pneumonitis with appreciable loss of ventilatory capacity is less well tolerated (Callaghan, 2015; Rogers, 2010).

220
00:27:01,961 --> 00:27:10,748
Hypoxemia and acidosis are also poorly accommodated by the fetus, and they may stimulate preterm labor after midpregnancy.

221
00:27:10,748 --> 00:27:19,961
Because many cases of pneumonia follow viral upper respiratory illnesses, worsening or persistence of symptoms may represent developing pneumonia.

222
00:27:19,961 --> 00:27:25,011
Any gravida suspected of having pneumonia should undergo chest radiography.

223
00:27:25,011 --> 00:27:27,711
# Bacterial Pneumonia.

224
00:27:27,711 --> 00:27:33,861
Many bacteria that cause CAP, such as S pneumoniae, are part of the normal flora.

225
00:27:33,861 --> 00:27:46,748
Some factors that perturb the symbiotic relationship between colonizing bacteria and mucosal phagocytic defenses include acquisition of a virulent strain or bacterial infections following a viral infection.

226
00:27:46,748 --> 00:27:55,486
Cigarette smoking and chronic bronchitis favor colonization with S pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, and Legionella species.

227
00:27:55,486 --> 00:28:03,198
Other risk factors include asthma, binge drinking, and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection .

228
00:28:03,198 --> 00:28:08,548
Incidence and Causes Pregnancy itself does not appear to predispose to pneumonia.

229
00:28:08,548 --> 00:28:23,048
Jin and associates (2003) reported the antepartum hospitalization rate for pneumonia to be 1.5 per 1000 deliveries—almost identical to the rate of 1.47 per 1000 for nonpregnant women.

230
00:28:23,048 --> 00:28:31,048
Similarly, among 75,000 pregnancies at Parkland Hospital, the incidence was 1.5 per 1000 .

231
00:28:31,048 --> 00:28:41,148
As discussed, at least half are caused by viruses—influenza A and B, metapneumovirus, adenovirus, and respiratory syncytial virus .

232
00:28:41,148 --> 00:28:45,711
A fourth are bacterial, and S pneumoniae causes half of these.

233
00:28:45,711 --> 00:28:55,723
Community-acquired methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (CA-MRSA) has emerged as a common pathogen that may cause necrotizing pneumonia .

234
00:28:55,723 --> 00:28:59,448
Occasionally, Legionnaires disease is encountered .

235
00:28:59,448 --> 00:29:07,023
Diagnosis Typical symptoms of pneumonia include cough, dyspnea, sputum production, and pleuritic chest pain.

236
00:29:07,023 --> 00:29:14,548
Mild upper respiratory symptoms and malaise precede these symptoms, and mild leukocytosis is usually present.

237
00:29:14,548 --> 00:29:18,223
Chest radiography is essential for diagnosis .

238
00:29:18,223 --> 00:29:27,373
Radiographical findings do not accurately predict the etiology, and as discussed, the responsible pathogen is often not identified.

239
00:29:27,373 --> 00:29:31,311
Tests to identify a specific agent are optional .

240
00:29:31,311 --> 00:29:43,961
Sputum cultures, polymerase chain reaction (PCR) tests, serological testing, cold agglutinin identification, and tests for bacterial antigens are not routinely recommended.

241
00:29:43,961 --> 00:29:49,761
Exceptions are rapid PCR testing of nasal swab samples for influenza A

242
00:29:49,924 --> 00:29:52,549
and B and for COVID-19.

243
00:29:52,499 --> 00:29:55,811
An assay to screen for all three is available.

244
00:29:55,811 --> 00:30:01,474
Determination of serum procalcitonin levels did not improve diagnostic efficacy .

245
00:30:01,474 --> 00:30:04,186
Figure 54-4.

246
00:30:04,186 --> 00:30:09,799
Chest radiograph in a pregnant woman with right middle lobe and left upper lobe pneumonia.

247
00:30:09,799 --> 00:30:15,574
Rounded right middle lobe and left apical infiltrates are consistent with the diagnosis.

248
00:30:15,574 --> 00:30:27,211
Management Many healthy young adults can be safely treated as outpatients, and the pneumonia severity index (PSI) and the CURB-65 scoring system are guides to admission .

249
00:30:27,211 --> 00:30:35,661
Neither has been studied in pregnancy, and at Parkland Hospital, we hospitalize all gravidas with radiographically proven pneumonia.

250
00:30:35,661 --> 00:30:42,261
Outpatient therapy or 23-hour observation with optimal followup are reasonable options.

251
00:30:42,261 --> 00:30:48,861
Table 54-4 displays risk factors that should prompt consideration for hospitalization .

252
00:30:48,861 --> 00:30:51,499
Table 54-4.

253
00:30:51,499 --> 00:31:01,411
Risk Factors for Early Deterioration in Community-Acquired Pneumonia With severe disease, admission to an ICU or intermediate care unit is advisable.

254
00:31:01,411 --> 00:31:08,711
Approximately 20 percent of pregnant women with pneumonia admitted to Parkland Hospital require this level of care .

255
00:31:08,711 --> 00:31:19,436
Severe pneumonia is a relatively common cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) during pregnancy, and mechanical ventilation may become necessary .

256
00:31:19,436 --> 00:31:26,086
In a review of 51 gravidas who required mechanical ventilation, 12 percent had pneumonia .

257
00:31:26,086 --> 00:31:31,411
Still, their mortality rate is lower than that for other causes of ARDS .

258
00:31:31,411 --> 00:31:36,049
Initial antimicrobial and antiviral treatment is empirical .

259
00:31:36,049 --> 00:31:44,386
In most otherwise healthy pregnant women, bacterial pneumonias are caused by pneumococci, Mycoplasma, or Chlamydophila.

260
00:31:44,386 --> 00:31:47,824
Monotherapy initially is with a macrolide .

261
00:31:47,824 --> 00:32:00,361
Yost and colleagues (2000) reported that erythromycin monotherapy, given intravenously and then orally, was effective in all but one of 99 pregnant women with uncomplicated pneumonia.

262
00:32:00,361 --> 00:32:09,211
During influenza season, we routinely administer oseltamivir (Tamiflu) along with empirical therapy for bacterial pneumonia.

263
00:32:09,211 --> 00:32:11,986
Table 54-5.

264
00:32:11,986 --> 00:32:32,586
Empirical Inpatient Antimicrobial Treatment for CommunityAcquired Pneumonia in Pregnancya For women with more severe disease according to criteria in Table 54-4, Mandell and Wunderink (2018) recommend: (1) a respiratory fluoroquinolone or (2) a macrolide plus a preferred β-lactam .

265
00:32:32,586 --> 00:32:38,524
Resistance of pneumococcal isolates to macrolides will favor fluoroquinolone regimens.

266
00:32:38,524 --> 00:32:44,636
The teratogenicity risk of fluoroquinolones is low, and these should be given if indicated .

267
00:32:44,636 --> 00:32:55,949
If MRSA is suspected, IV vancomycin, 15 mg/kg every 12 hours, or linezolid, 600 mg every 12 hours, is added .

268
00:32:55,949 --> 00:33:03,574
Clinical improvement is usually evident in 48 to 72 hours with resolution of fever in 2 to 4 days.

269
00:33:03,574 --> 00:33:08,086
With improvement, patients can be transitioned to oral forms.

270
00:33:08,086 --> 00:33:13,636
Treatment of uncomplicated pneumonia is recommended for 5 to 7 days total .

271
00:33:13,636 --> 00:33:22,286
Patients should be afebrile for 48 to 72 hours and demonstrate signs of clinical stability before therapy is discontinued.

272
00:33:22,286 --> 00:33:27,561
Radiographic abnormalities may take up to 6 weeks to completely resolve .

273
00:33:27,561 --> 00:33:34,636
Worsening disease is a poor prognostic feature, and serial radiography is recommended if fever persists.

274
00:33:34,636 --> 00:33:40,911
Even with improvement, however, approximately 20 percent of women develop a pleural effusion.

275
00:33:40,911 --> 00:33:50,524
Treatment failure may occur in up to 15 percent of cases, and a wider antimicrobial regimen and more extensive diagnostic testing is warranted.

276
00:33:50,524 --> 00:33:59,124
For example, in some cases, computed tomography of the chest may be necessary to identify empyema that requires drainage.

277
00:33:59,124 --> 00:34:05,786
Pregnancy Outcome Maternal and perinatal morbidity and mortality rates from pneumonia remain formidable.

278
00:34:05,786 --> 00:34:19,049
In five studies with a total of 632 women published after 1990, almost 7 percent required intubation and mechanical ventilation, and the maternal mortality rate was 0.8 percent.

279
00:34:19,049 --> 00:34:31,986
In up to a third of cases of acute lung infection, prematurely ruptured membranes and preterm delivery are frequent complications (Getahun, 2007; Shariatzadeh, 2006).

280
00:34:31,986 --> 00:34:38,611
Likely related to these, older studies report a twofold increase in rates of low-birthweight neonates .

281
00:34:38,611 --> 00:34:46,249
In one population-based study from Taiwan of nearly 219,000 births, incidences of preterm

282
00:34:46,420 --> 00:34:52,820
and growth-restricted newborns and of preeclampsia and cesarean delivery were significantly higher .

283
00:34:52,770 --> 00:35:06,570
Prevention Two pneumococcal vaccines are available—a 23-serotype preparation (PPSV23 [Pneumovax]) and a newer 13-serotype vaccine (PCV13 [Prevnar 13]).

284
00:35:06,570 --> 00:35:14,795
PPSV23 is 60- to 70-percent protective, and its use lowers emergence of drug-resistant pneumococci.

285
00:35:14,795 --> 00:35:18,807
Neither vaccine is recommended for healthy pregnant women.

286
00:35:18,807 --> 00:35:31,232
However, for gravidas with chronic diabetes mellitus or with chronic heart, lung, or liver disease, one lifetime dose of PPSV23 is suggested, if not previously provided.

287
00:35:31,232 --> 00:35:51,895
Moreover, for women with chronic immunocompromise, generalized malignancy, chronic renal disease, cochlear implant, or asplenia, such as with sickle-cell disease, one lifetime PCV13 dose is followed by one PPSV23 dose at least 8 weeks later and then again 5 years later if not provided previously .

288
00:35:51,895 --> 00:35:54,682
# Influenza Pneumonia.

289
00:35:54,682 --> 00:36:04,082
Influenza A and B are RNA viruses that cause potentially epidemic respiratory infection, including pneumonitis, in the winter months.

290
00:36:04,082 --> 00:36:14,532
The virus is spread by aerosolized droplets and quickly infects ciliated columnar epithelium, alveolar cells, mucus gland cells, and macrophages.

291
00:36:14,532 --> 00:36:20,507
Clinical Presentation Each year, 10 percent of pregnant women develop influenza .

292
00:36:20,507 --> 00:36:29,107
Disease onset follows 1 to 4 days after exposure, and common symptoms include fever, cough, myalgia, and chills .

293
00:36:29,107 --> 00:36:32,982
In most healthy adults, infection is self-limited.

294
00:36:32,982 --> 00:36:37,982
Pneumonia is the most frequent complication and mimics bacterial pneumonia.

295
00:36:37,982 --> 00:36:45,445
According to the CDC, infected pregnant women are more likely to be hospitalized and admitted to an ICU .

296
00:36:45,445 --> 00:36:52,645
Others have corroborated these observations (Mertz, 2019; Prasad, 2019).

297
00:36:52,645 --> 00:37:02,045
At Parkland Hospital during the 2003–2004 influenza season, pneumonia developed in 12 percent of pregnant women with influenza .

298
00:37:02,045 --> 00:37:11,970
The 2009–2010 pandemic with the influenza (A/H1N1) pdm09 strain was particularly severe .

299
00:37:11,970 --> 00:37:23,895
In an MFMU Network study, 10 percent of pregnant or postpartum women admitted with H1N1 influenza were cared for in an ICU, and 11 percent of these patients died .

300
00:37:23,895 --> 00:37:29,582
Risk factors included advanced pregnancy, smoking, and chronic hypertension.

301
00:37:29,582 --> 00:37:38,245
Overall, influenza accounted for 12 percent of pregnancyrelated deaths during the 2009–2010 pandemic .

302
00:37:38,245 --> 00:37:46,920
During the 2013–2014 influenza season, a fourth of pregnant women admitted to California ICUs died .

303
00:37:46,920 --> 00:37:53,707
ECMO may be lifesaving (Anselmi, 2015; Saad, 2016).

304
00:37:53,707 --> 00:37:59,682
Rapid PCR testing of nasal swab samples for influenza A and B is available.

305
00:37:59,682 --> 00:38:07,570
Primary influenza pneumonitis is characterized by sparse sputum production and radiographic interstitial infiltrates .

306
00:38:07,570 --> 00:38:19,007
More commonly, secondary or mixed pneumonia develop from bacterial superinfection by streptococci or staphylococci after 2 to 3 days of initial clinical improvement.

307
00:38:19,007 --> 00:38:29,457
The CDC (2007) reported several cases in which CAMRSA caused influenza-associated pneumonitis with a case-fatality rate of 25 percent.

308
00:38:29,457 --> 00:38:41,245
Management Supportive treatment is recommended for uncomplicated influenza, and early antiviral therapy is effective (Oboho, 2016; Wright, 2018).

309
00:38:41,245 --> 00:38:51,395
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018a) recommends treatment of all pregnant women with suspected or proven influenza.

310
00:38:51,395 --> 00:38:57,157
This is regardless of rapid influenza testing, which can have false-negative results .

311
00:38:57,157 --> 00:39:02,407
Hospitalization is considered for severely ill women and for those with pneumonia.

312
00:39:02,407 --> 00:39:13,120
Influenza hospitalization rates for those with advanced pregnancy are higher compared with nonpregnant women (Dodds, 2007; Schanzer, 2007).

313
00:39:13,120 --> 00:39:23,007
Treatment is with neuraminidase inhibitors, which interfere with release of progeny virus from infected host cells and thus prevent infection of new host cells.

314
00:39:23,007 --> 00:39:30,482
The preferred agent is oseltamivir (Tamiflu), which is given orally, 75 mg twice daily.

315
00:39:30,482 --> 00:39:37,395
An alternative is zanamivir (Relenza), which is given by inhalation, 10 mg twice daily.

316
00:39:37,395 --> 00:39:41,282
Recommended treatment duration with either is 5 days.

317
00:39:41,282 --> 00:39:48,970
Last, peramivir (Rapivab) may be given as a 600-mg IV dose over 15 to 30 minutes.

318
00:39:48,970 --> 00:39:57,382
All shorten the course of illness by 1 to 2 days, and they probably reduce the risk for pneumonitis (Belgi, 2014;

319
00:39:57,532 --> 00:40:00,482
Muthuri, 2014).

320
00:40:00,432 --> 00:40:08,857
Few data guide use of these agents in pregnant women, but the drugs are not teratogenic in animal studies and are considered low risk .

321
00:40:08,857 --> 00:40:19,469
Recently, the endonuclease inhibitor baloxavir marboxil (Xofluza) was approved for use in nonpregnant individuals but is not recommended for pregnant women.

322
00:40:19,469 --> 00:40:32,757
Prevention Vaccination against influenza A is recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b) and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2019a).

323
00:40:32,757 --> 00:40:41,869
During the 2018–2019 flu season, the CDC reported that only half of pregnant women received the influenza vaccine .

324
00:40:41,869 --> 00:40:51,119
In a recent survey, however, O’Leary and colleagues (2019) reported a 10-percent vaccine refusal rate in office practices.

325
00:40:51,119 --> 00:41:01,607
Any inactivated influenza vaccines (IIV) or recombinant influenza vaccine (RIV) may be used and is given once each flu season.

326
00:41:01,607 --> 00:41:07,507
Live attenuated influenza vaccine (LAIV) should not be used in pregnancy .

327
00:41:07,507 --> 00:41:18,257
Prenatal vaccination affords some temporary protection for infants (Madhi, 2014; Munoz, 2019; Tita, 2016).

328
00:41:18,257 --> 00:41:30,469
The vaccine has not been linked with birth defects or adverse childhood health effects at 5 years of age (Briggs, 2022; Conlin, 2018; Walsh, 2019).

329
00:41:30,469 --> 00:41:36,357
In one case-control study, an association with vaccination and miscarriage was found .

330
00:41:36,357 --> 00:41:46,032
However, the observations were not definite and should not alter clinical practice (Rasmussen, 2019; Sperling, 2018).

331
00:41:46,032 --> 00:41:53,257
Effects on pregnancy outcomes are discussed in more detail in Chapter 67 (p. 1187).

332
00:41:53,257 --> 00:42:02,069
A second coronavirus—Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERSCoV)—originated in Saudi Arabia in 2013.

333
00:42:02,069 --> 00:42:06,157
Fortunately, the epidemic was contained and short lived.

334
00:42:06,157 --> 00:42:08,669
# Coronavirus.

335
00:42:08,669 --> 00:42:17,957
The first coronaviral infection in China in 2002 that caused severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) was termed SARS-CoV.

336
00:42:17,957 --> 00:42:23,657
It caused atypical pneumonitis with a case-fatality rate of approximately 10 percent .

337
00:42:23,657 --> 00:42:28,344
SARS in pregnancy had a case-fatality rate of up to 40 percent .

338
00:42:28,344 --> 00:42:42,144
Ng and associates (2006) reported that the placentas from 7 of 19 cases showed abnormal intervillous or subchorionic fibrin deposition in three, and extensive fetal thrombotic vasculopathy in two.

339
00:42:42,144 --> 00:42:50,694
In the winter of 2019–2020, a second worldwide outbreak of a genetically similar virus originated in China.

340
00:42:50,694 --> 00:42:56,194
This was Coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV2) known as COVID-19.

341
00:42:56,194 --> 00:43:03,344
Illness severity ranges from mild common-cold symptoms to severe pneumonia or acute respiratory distress syndrome .

342
00:43:03,344 --> 00:43:10,744
From 18 affected pregnancies, the clinical course of COVID-19 is similar to that seen in nonpregnant women.

343
00:43:10,744 --> 00:43:18,032
However, preterm labor appears to be an associated risk (Chen, 2020; Zhu, 2020).

344
00:43:18,032 --> 00:43:31,432
To minimize transmission of COVID-19, inpatient guidelines recommend caring for an affected patient in a negative-pressure airborne infection isolation room (AIIR) or transfer to an equipped facility.

345
00:43:31,432 --> 00:43:39,444
The potential for vertical transmission (mother–to–newborn) transmission is currently unclear Breslin, 2020).

346
00:43:39,444 --> 00:43:42,569
Neonates are suspected to have infection.

347
00:43:42,569 --> 00:43:47,219
Thus, isolation of the newborn from other neonates is recommended.

348
00:43:47,219 --> 00:44:00,857
A newborn may room in with its mother, who practices hand hygiene and wears a mask (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021; Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 2020).

349
00:44:00,857 --> 00:44:03,532
# Varicella Pneumonia.

350
00:44:03,532 --> 00:44:12,432
Infection with varicella-zoster virus, the same agent responsible for chickenpox, results in pneumonitis in 5 percent of gravidas .

351
00:44:12,432 --> 00:44:18,532
Diagnosis and management are considered in Chapter 67 (p. 1186).

352
00:44:18,532 --> 00:44:21,357
# Pneumocystis Pneumonia.

353
00:44:21,357 --> 00:44:31,519
Fungal and yeast infections are usually of greatest consequence in immunocompromised hosts, especially in women with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).

354
00:44:31,519 --> 00:44:39,719
Of these, lung infection with Pneumocystis jirovecii, formerly called Pneumocystis carinii, is a common complication.

355
00:44:39,719 --> 00:44:50,119
The opportunistic yeast-like fungus causes interstitial pneumonia characterized by dry cough, tachypnea, dyspnea, and diffuse radiographic infiltrates.

356
00:44:50,119 --> 00:45:01,094
This organism is identified by polymerase chain reaction testing of bronchoalveolar lavage secretions or, when necessary, tissue staining of biopsy specimens .

357
00:45:01,094 --> 00:45:04,869
More common in HIVinfected persons, its

358
00:45:05,020 --> 00:45:09,007
incidence has risen in other immunocompromised groups .

359
00:45:08,957 --> 00:45:17,457
In a report from the AIDS Clinical Trials Centers, pneumocystis pneumonia was the most frequent HIV-related disorder in pregnant women .

360
00:45:17,457 --> 00:45:25,570
Ahmad and associates (2001) reviewed 22 cases during pregnancy and cited a 50-percent mortality rate.

361
00:45:25,570 --> 00:45:30,970
Treatment is with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for 14 to 21 days.

362
00:45:30,970 --> 00:45:35,545
Alternative agents include sulfadiazine plus pyrimethamine.

363
00:45:35,545 --> 00:45:39,257
Glucocorticoids also improve survival rates.

364
00:45:39,257 --> 00:45:43,995
Echinocandins have been used successfully in nonpregnant patients .

365
00:45:43,995 --> 00:45:49,395
Tracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation may be required in some cases.

366
00:45:49,395 --> 00:46:01,070
For prophylaxis, several international health agencies recommend one double-strength trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole tablet orally daily for certain groups of HIV-infected pregnant women.

367
00:46:01,070 --> 00:46:16,632
These include women with CD4+ Tlymphocyte counts <200/μL, those whose CD4+ T lymphocytes constitute <14 percent, or if there is an AIDS-defining illness, particularly oropharyngeal candidiasis .

368
00:46:16,632 --> 00:46:19,120
# Fungal Pneumonia.

369
00:46:19,120 --> 00:46:22,370
Any of several fungi can cause pneumonia.

370
00:46:22,370 --> 00:46:29,520
In pregnancy, this is usually seen in women with HIV infection or other sources of immunocompromise.

371
00:46:29,520 --> 00:46:34,670
In uncompromised women, infection is usually mild and self-limited.

372
00:46:34,670 --> 00:46:40,157
It is characterized initially by cough and fever, and dissemination is infrequent .

373
00:46:40,157 --> 00:46:47,045
Histoplasmosis and blastomycosis do not appear to be more frequent or more severe during pregnancy .

374
00:46:47,045 --> 00:46:55,132
Data concerning coccidioidomycosis are conflicting (Bercovitch, 2011; Patel, 2013).

375
00:46:55,132 --> 00:47:02,445
In a case-control study from an endemic area, pregnancy was a significant risk factor for disseminated disease .

376
00:47:02,445 --> 00:47:14,195
In another study, however, Caldwell and coworkers (2000) identified 32 serologically confirmed cases during pregnancy and documented dissemination in only three.

377
00:47:14,195 --> 00:47:23,995
Women with associated erythema nodosum have a better prognosis, whereas mediastinal lymphadenopathy may more likely reflect disseminated disease .

378
00:47:23,995 --> 00:47:36,670
Last, Crum and Ballon-Landa (2006) reviewed 80 cases of antepartum coccidioidomycosis and found that almost all women diagnosed in the third trimester had disseminated disease.

379
00:47:36,670 --> 00:47:45,595
Although the overall maternal mortality rate was 40 percent, it was 20 percent for 29 cases reported since 1973.

380
00:47:45,595 --> 00:47:54,932
Spinello (2007) and Bercovitch (2011) with their colleagues provide reviews of coccidioidomycosis in pregnancy.

381
00:47:54,932 --> 00:48:00,745
Most cases of cryptococcosis reported during pregnancy manifest as meningitis.

382
00:48:00,745 --> 00:48:05,557
Otherwise healthy pregnant women occasionally have cryptococcal pneumonia .

383
00:48:05,557 --> 00:48:12,407
Diagnosis is difficult because clinical presentation is similar to that of other community-acquired pneumonias.

384
00:48:12,407 --> 00:48:17,745
Itraconazole is the preferred therapy for most disseminated fungal infections .

385
00:48:17,745 --> 00:48:28,757
Pregnant women have also been given IV amphotericin B or ketoconazole (Lewis, 2018; Paranyuk, 2006; Pilmis, 2015).

386
00:48:28,757 --> 00:48:34,707
Amphotericin B has been used extensively in pregnancy with no embryofetal effects.

387
00:48:34,707 --> 00:48:41,270
Early in pregnancy, fluconazole and ketoconazole may be embryotoxic in large doses.

388
00:48:41,270 --> 00:48:47,032
In the first trimester, these two and itraconazole are ideally avoided if possible .

389
00:48:47,032 --> 00:48:55,607
Three echinocandin derivatives—caspofungin, micafungin, and anidulafungin—are effective for invasive candidiasis .

390
00:48:55,607 --> 00:49:02,845
They are embryotoxic and teratogenic in laboratory animals and use in human pregnancies has not been reported .

391
00:49:02,845 --> 00:49:04,820
Tuberculosis.

392
00:49:04,820 --> 00:49:11,395
It is estimated that a third of the world population is infected with Mycobacterium tuberculosis .

393
00:49:11,395 --> 00:49:20,432
In the United States, it is uncommon and is a disease of the elderly, the urban poor, minority groups, and patients with HIV infection .

394
00:49:20,432 --> 00:49:32,195
According to the CDC (2019b), the incidence of active tuberculosis in this country has plateaued since 2000, and immigrants account for more than half of active cases.

395
00:49:32,195 --> 00:49:41,295
Individuals born in the United States have newly acquired infection, whereas foreign-born persons usually have reactivation of latent infection.

396
00:49:41,295 --> 00:49:48,520
Infection begins with inhalation of mycobacteria, which incites a granulomatous pulmonary reaction.

397
00:49:48,520 --> 00:49:54,920
In >90 percent of patients, infection is contained and is dormant for long periods .

398
00:49:54,920 --> 00:50:00,382
In those who are immunocompromised, tuberculosis becomes reactivated to

399
00:50:00,532 --> 00:50:02,794
cause clinical disease.

400
00:50:02,744 --> 00:50:10,807
Active disease manifests usually as cough with minimal sputum production, low-grade fever, hemoptysis, and weight loss.

401
00:50:10,807 --> 00:50:19,582
Various infiltrative patterns are seen on chest radiograph, and cavitation or mediastinal lymphadenopathy may be associated.

402
00:50:19,582 --> 00:50:28,232
Stained smears of sputum reveal acid-fast bacilli (AFB) in approximately two thirds of culturepositive individuals.

403
00:50:28,232 --> 00:50:39,669
Forms of extrapulmonary tuberculosis include lymphadenitis, pleural, genitourinary, skeletal, meningeal, gastrointestinal, and miliary or disseminated .

404
00:50:39,669 --> 00:50:44,344
Silent endometrial tuberculosis can cause tubal infertility .

405
00:50:44,344 --> 00:50:47,607
# Tuberculosis and Pregnancy.

406
00:50:47,607 --> 00:50:59,432
The considerable influx of women into the United States from Asia, Africa, Mexico, and Central America has been accompanied by an increased frequency of tuberculosis in pregnancy.

407
00:50:59,432 --> 00:51:07,044
The prevalence of tuberculosis was 7.1 cases per 100,000 pregnancy-related hospitalizations .

408
00:51:07,044 --> 00:51:18,307
Sackoff and associates (2006) reported positive tuberculin test results in half of 678 foreign-born women attending prenatal clinics in New York City.

409
00:51:18,307 --> 00:51:28,019
At Jackson Memorial Hospital in Miami, 21 percent of 207 pregnant women with HIV infection had a positive skin test result .

410
00:51:28,019 --> 00:51:35,782
One review of 3384 pregnant women showed that 72 percent of active disease is pulmonary .

411
00:51:35,782 --> 00:51:41,969
It also confirmed earlier studies that showed greater adverse maternal and perinatal outcomes .

412
00:51:41,969 --> 00:51:49,119
Others have found similar untoward effects (Dennis, 2018; El-Messidi, 2016).

413
00:51:49,119 --> 00:51:57,969
From her review, Efferen (2007) cited twofold greater rates of low birthweight, preterm delivery, and preeclampsia.

414
00:51:57,969 --> 00:52:02,082
The perinatal mortality rate was almost tenfold higher.

415
00:52:02,082 --> 00:52:11,994
Conversely, treated tuberculosis is usually associated with good pregnancy outcomes (Moro, 2018; Taylor, 2013).

416
00:52:11,994 --> 00:52:14,732
Table 54-6.

417
00:52:14,732 --> 00:52:27,219
Adverse Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes in 3384 Pregnancies Complicated by Active Tuberculosis Extrapulmonary tuberculosis is less common, although its incidence appears to be rising .

418
00:52:27,219 --> 00:52:35,682
Llewelyn and colleagues (2000) reported that 9 of 13 pregnant women with extrapulmonary disease had delayed diagnoses.

419
00:52:35,682 --> 00:52:43,544
In the review cited above, 5.8 percent of women had extrapulmonary infections, and they had worse outcomes .

420
00:52:43,544 --> 00:52:55,532
Jana and coworkers (1999) described outcomes in 33 pregnant women with renal, intestinal, and skeletal tuberculosis, and a third had low-birthweight newborns.

421
00:52:55,532 --> 00:53:04,257
Prevost and Fung Kee Fung (1999) reviewed 56 cases of tuberculous meningitis in which a third of mothers died.

422
00:53:04,257 --> 00:53:11,219
Spinal tuberculosis may cause paraplegia, but vertebral fusion may prevent it from becoming permanent .

423
00:53:11,219 --> 00:53:16,157
Psoas abscess develops in 5 percent of those with spinal infections .

424
00:53:16,157 --> 00:53:34,857
Other presentations include widespread intraperitoneal tuberculosis simulating ovarian carcinomatosis or degenerating leiomyoma, and hyperemesis gravidarum stemming from tubercular meningitis (Kutlu, 2007; Moore, 2008; Sherer, 2005).

425
00:53:34,857 --> 00:53:37,257
# Diagnosis.

426
00:53:37,257 --> 00:53:41,457
For latent tuberculosis, two testing types are used.

427
00:53:41,457 --> 00:53:55,507
One is the time-honored tuberculin skin test (TST), and the others are interferon-gamma release assays (IGRAs), which are becoming preferred (Lewinsohn, 2017; Sosa, 2019).

428
00:53:55,507 --> 00:54:06,594
IGRAs are blood tests that measure interferon-gamma release in response to antigens present in M tuberculosis but not in bacille CalmetteGuérin (BCG) vaccine.

429
00:54:06,594 --> 00:54:17,244
The alternative is skin testing, for which the preferred antigen is purified protein derivative (PPD) of intermediate strength of 5 tuberculin units.

430
00:54:17,244 --> 00:54:23,619
If the intracutaneously applied test result is negative, no further evaluation is needed.

431
00:54:23,619 --> 00:54:34,394
A positive skin test result measures ≥5 mm in diameter and requires evaluation for active disease, including a chest radiograph.

432
00:54:34,394 --> 00:54:41,644
For active infection, nucleic acid amplification testing and mycobacterial culture is recommended .

433
00:54:41,644 --> 00:54:49,132
Clinicians should promptly contact their state health department for cases of suspected or confirmed active tuberculosis.

434
00:54:49,132 --> 00:54:54,107
Health departments can assist in providing patient and public health management.

435
00:54:54,107 --> 00:55:00,544
This often includes directly observed therapy (DOT) to ensure regimen compliance .

436
00:55:00,544 --> 00:55:10,369
Each state’s control office can be found at www.cdc.gov/tb/links/tboffices.htm.

437
00:55:10,369 --> 00:55:12,444
# Treatment.

438
00:55:12,444 --> 00:55:13,982
Latent

439
00:55:14,140 --> 00:55:27,677
Infection In nonpregnant, tuberculin-positive patients younger than 35 years with no evidence of active disease, treatment is given to substantially reduce the risk that their latent infection will progress to active disease.

440
00:55:27,627 --> 00:55:35,315
The preferred therapy is one of three rifamycin-based regimens with or without isoniazid for 3 to 4 months duration .

441
00:55:35,315 --> 00:55:42,377
Alternatively, isoniazid, 300 mg orally daily, is given for 6 to 9 months.

442
00:55:42,377 --> 00:55:51,302
Isoniazid has been used for decades, and it is considered safe in pregnancy (Briggs, 2022; Moro, 2018).

443
00:55:51,302 --> 00:55:59,352
However, for gravidas with latent infection, most recommend that isoniazid therapy be delayed until after delivery .

444
00:55:59,352 --> 00:56:09,490
Some even recommend withholding treatment until 3 to 6 months after delivery because of a possibly increased isoniazid-induced hepatitis risk in postpartum women.

445
00:56:09,490 --> 00:56:15,252
Serum levels of hepatic transaminases should be monitored with isoniazid therapy.

446
00:56:15,252 --> 00:56:19,177
Compliance is a major problem with postpartum therapy.

447
00:56:19,177 --> 00:56:27,827
Only 10 to 18 percent of postpartum patients complete treatment (Cruz, 2005; Sackoff, 2006).

448
00:56:27,827 --> 00:56:34,590
One obvious disconnect is that care for tuberculosis is given in health systems apart from prenatal care .

449
00:56:34,590 --> 00:56:45,615
In a decision model, Boggess and colleagues (2000) reported fewer cases of tuberculosis if treatment for a positive tuberculin skin test result occurred antepartum.

450
00:56:45,615 --> 00:56:50,477
There are exceptions to delayed treatment for latent infection in pregnancy.

451
00:56:50,477 --> 00:56:58,127
Recent skin test convertors are treated antepartum because the incidence of active infection is 5 percent in the first year .

452
00:56:58,127 --> 00:57:06,915
Skin-test-positive women exposed to active infection are also treated because the incidence of infection is 0.5 percent per year.

453
00:57:06,915 --> 00:57:14,640
Last, women with HIV infection are treated because they have an approximate 10-percent annual risk of active disease.

454
00:57:14,640 --> 00:57:17,215
Active Infection Regimens.

455
00:57:17,215 --> 00:57:25,827
Resistance to antituberculosis drugs has led to emergence of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB) strains.

456
00:57:25,827 --> 00:57:36,865
The World Health Organization (2017) recommends isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol, and pyrazinamide together for 6 months for nonpregnant persons.

457
00:57:36,865 --> 00:57:40,852
These are used until susceptibility studies are performed .

458
00:57:40,852 --> 00:57:46,052
Pyridoxine is added to help prevent isoniazid-associated neuropathy.

459
00:57:46,052 --> 00:57:54,615
A newer 4-month isoniazid, rifapentine, moxifloxacin, and pyrazinamide regimen was not inferior to the standard one .

460
00:57:54,615 --> 00:58:02,365
During pregnancy, in the first 2-month bactericidal phase, isoniazid, rifampin, and ethambutol are given.

461
00:58:02,365 --> 00:58:07,940
This is followed by a 7-month continuation phase with isoniazid and rifampin .

462
00:58:07,940 --> 00:58:14,377
A few reports describe MDR-TB during pregnancy, and treatment options have been reviewed .

463
00:58:14,377 --> 00:58:20,452
Treatment should be undertaken in consultation with infectious disease and the local health department.

464
00:58:20,452 --> 00:58:24,865
Breastfeeding is not prohibited during antituberculous therapy.

465
00:58:24,865 --> 00:58:31,215
If neonatal therapy is indicated, medication levels in breast milk are inadequate for this.

466
00:58:31,215 --> 00:58:38,052
Treatment of active disease is of special concern in HIV-infected women who are antiretroviral naïve.

467
00:58:38,052 --> 00:58:51,015
In these circumstances, beginning concomitant therapy with antituberculosis and antiretroviral therapy can cause the immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) with toxic drug effects .

468
00:58:51,015 --> 00:59:03,402
That said, some studies support administration of antiretroviral therapy 2 to 4 weeks after beginning antituberculosis therapy (Blanc, 2011; Havlir, 2011).

469
00:59:03,402 --> 00:59:13,090
Also, rifampin or rifabutin may be contraindicated if certain protease inhibitors or nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors are used.

470
00:59:13,090 --> 00:59:19,027
If there is resistance to rifabutin or rifampin, then pyrazinamide therapy is given.

471
00:59:19,027 --> 00:59:30,090
Of the second-line regimens, the aminoglycosides—streptomycin, kanamycin, amikacin, and capreomycin —are ototoxic to the fetus and are contraindicated .

472
00:59:30,090 --> 00:59:32,427
Transmission Prevention.

473
00:59:32,427 --> 00:59:42,752
Inpatient airborne precautions should be initiated for any patient with suspected active pulmonary tuberculosis or for patients who remain infectious despite treatment.

474
00:59:42,752 --> 00:59:46,515
Precautions include rooming in a negative-pressure room.

475
00:59:46,515 --> 00:59:55,915
Patients are encouraged to wear surgical masks during interactions, and health-care workers should don personally fitted N95 respirator masks.

476
00:59:55,915 --> 00:59:58,815
Patients undergoing treatment can be

477
00:59:58,972 --> 01:00:13,909
considered noninfectious: (1) after 2 weeks of DOT treatment compliance, (2) after three consecutive AFB-negative sputum smears, or (3) after clinical improvement evidenced by absent fever and resolving cough .

478
01:00:13,859 --> 01:00:21,822
Neonatal Tuberculosis Tuberculous bacillemia can infect the placenta, but the fetus infrequently becomes infected.

479
01:00:21,822 --> 01:00:29,622
Congenital tuberculosis applies also to newborns who are infected by aspiration of infected secretions at delivery.

480
01:00:29,622 --> 01:00:33,647
Each route constitutes approximately half of the cases.

481
01:00:33,647 --> 01:00:43,972
Neonatal tuberculosis simulates other congenital infections and manifests with hepatosplenomegaly, respiratory distress, fever, and lymphadenopathy .

482
01:00:43,972 --> 01:00:50,747
Cantwell and colleagues (1994) reviewed 29 cases of congenital tuberculosis.

483
01:00:50,747 --> 01:00:59,359
Only 12 of the mothers had active infection, and tuberculosis was frequently demonstrated by postpartum endometrial biopsy.

484
01:00:59,359 --> 01:01:08,822
Adhikari and associates (1997) described 11 South African postpartum women whose endometrial biopsy was culture-positive.

485
01:01:08,822 --> 01:01:12,659
Six of their neonates had congenital tuberculosis.

486
01:01:12,659 --> 01:01:20,572
Neonatal infection is unlikely if the mother with active disease is treated before delivery or if her sputum culture is negative.

487
01:01:20,572 --> 01:01:27,647
Most experts recommend isolation of the susceptible newborn from the mother suspected of having active disease.

488
01:01:27,647 --> 01:01:34,159
If the mother is infectious, 3 to 6 months of isoniazid prophylaxis is given to the newborn.

489
01:01:34,159 --> 01:01:42,472
In an older study, the risk of disease in the untreated infant born to a woman with active infection was 50 percent in the first year .

490
01:01:42,472 --> 01:01:44,459
Sarcoidosis.

491
01:01:44,459 --> 01:02:00,497
This is a chronic, multisystem inflammatory disease of unknown etiology characterized by an accumulation of T-helper lymphocytes and phagocytes within noncaseating granulomas (Baughman, 2018; Spagnolo, 2018).

492
01:02:00,497 --> 01:02:09,797
Predisposition to the disease is genetically determined and characterized by an exaggerated response of helper T lymphocytes to environmental triggers.

493
01:02:09,797 --> 01:02:17,572
Pulmonary involvement is most common, followed by skin, eyes, lymph nodes, and then all other organ systems.

494
01:02:17,572 --> 01:02:25,509
The prevalence of sarcoid in the United States is 20 to 60 cases per 100,000, with equal sex distribution.

495
01:02:25,509 --> 01:02:29,809
It is more than 10 times more common for blacks than for whites.

496
01:02:29,809 --> 01:02:34,659
Most patients are between 20 and 40 years at disease presentation.

497
01:02:34,659 --> 01:02:42,284
Pulmonary symptoms are dominant, and more than 90 percent of patients have an abnormal chest radiograph at some point.

498
01:02:42,284 --> 01:02:50,884
Interstitial pneumonitis is the hallmark of pulmonary involvement, and half of affected patients develop permanent radiological changes.

499
01:02:50,884 --> 01:02:58,297
Lymphadenopathy, especially of the mediastinum, is present in 75 to 90 percent of cases.

500
01:02:58,297 --> 01:03:05,147
A third each have uveitis and skin involvement, the latter usually manifest as erythema nodosum.

501
01:03:05,147 --> 01:03:11,184
In women, sarcoid causes approximately 10 percent of cases of erythema nodosum .

502
01:03:11,184 --> 01:03:15,297
Importantly, any other organ system may be involved.

503
01:03:15,297 --> 01:03:20,497
Confirmation of the diagnosis is with biopsy—preferably a lymph node.

504
01:03:20,497 --> 01:03:27,647
However, because the lung may be the only obviously involved organ, tissue acquisition is often difficult.

505
01:03:27,647 --> 01:03:35,084
The overall prognosis for sarcoidosis is good, and it resolves without treatment in 50 percent of patients.

506
01:03:35,084 --> 01:03:43,659
Still, quality of life is diminished, and in 20 percent chronic organ dysfunction, albeit mild and nonprogressive, persists .

507
01:03:43,659 --> 01:03:47,559
Approximately 5 percent die because of their disease.

508
01:03:47,559 --> 01:03:52,634
Glucocorticoids are the most widely used treatment for symptomatic disease.

509
01:03:52,634 --> 01:03:56,809
Permanent organ derangement is seldom reversed by their use .

510
01:03:56,809 --> 01:04:04,797
Thus, the decision to treat is based on symptoms, physical findings, chest radiograph, and pulmonary function tests.

511
01:04:04,797 --> 01:04:11,947
Unless respiratory symptoms are prominent, therapy is usually withheld for a several-month observation period.

512
01:04:11,947 --> 01:04:22,047
If inflammation does not subside, prednisone, 20 to 40 mg/d, is given daily and tapered to <10 mg by 6 months .

513
01:04:22,047 --> 01:04:29,997
For those with an inadequate response, immunosuppressive or cytotoxic agents and cytokine modulators can be used.

514
01:04:29,997 --> 01:04:33,272
# Sarcoidosis and Pregnancy.

515
01:04:33,272 --> 01:04:39,984
Because it is uncommon and frequently benign, sarcoidosis is not often diagnosed in pregnancy.

516
01:04:39,984 --> 01:04:47,634
In a study of the Nationwide Inpatient Sample, the incidence was 9.6 cases per 100,000 births .

517
01:04:47,634 --> 01:04:50,047
Meningitis, heart

518
01:04:50,212 --> 01:05:00,937
failure, and neurosarcoidosis complicating pregnancy have been described (Cardonick, 2000; Maisel, 1996; Wallmüller, 2012).

519
01:05:00,887 --> 01:05:06,737
Many small studies report benign associations between sarcoidosis and pregnancy.

520
01:05:06,737 --> 01:05:10,087
However, Table 54-7.

521
01:05:10,087 --> 01:05:19,937
displays the increased incidences of both maternal and fetal adverse outcomes found in a Nationwide Inpatient Sample study of 678 cases .

522
01:05:19,937 --> 01:05:25,424
Risks for preeclampsia and preterm delivery were approximately twofold greater.

523
01:05:25,424 --> 01:05:32,562
Risks for eclampsia, deep-vein thrombosis, and pulmonary embolism were five- to sevenfold higher.

524
01:05:32,562 --> 01:05:35,274
TABLE 54-7.

525
01:05:35,274 --> 01:05:45,749
Maternal and Perinatal Adverse Outcomes in 678 Women with Sarcoidosis Active sarcoidosis is treated using the same guidelines as for nongravid women.

526
01:05:45,749 --> 01:05:50,349
Severe disease warrants serial pulmonary function determination.

527
01:05:50,349 --> 01:05:57,299
Symptomatic uveitis, constitutional symptoms, and pulmonary symptoms are treated with prednisone.

528
01:05:57,299 --> 01:05:59,524
Cystic Fibrosis.

529
01:05:59,524 --> 01:06:02,324
# Pathophysiology.

530
01:06:02,324 --> 01:06:10,524
Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive exocrinopathy and is one of the most common fatal genetic disorders in Caucasians.

531
01:06:10,524 --> 01:06:18,849
Cystic fibrosis is caused by one of more than 2000 mutations in a 230-kb gene on the long arm of chromosome 7 .

532
01:06:18,849 --> 01:06:28,099
The normal peptide gene product functions as a chloride channel and is termed the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR).

533
01:06:28,099 --> 01:06:35,399
Mutations in the chloride channel alters epithelial transport of electrolytes in CFTR-secretory cells.

534
01:06:35,399 --> 01:06:41,874
Affected sites include the sinuses, lung, pancreas, liver, and reproductive tract.

535
01:06:41,874 --> 01:06:47,237
Exocrine gland ductal obstruction develops from thick, viscid secretions.

536
01:06:47,237 --> 01:06:51,362
In the lung, submucosal glandular ducts are affected.

537
01:06:51,362 --> 01:07:01,674
Eccrine sweat gland abnormalities are the basis for the diagnostic sweat test, characterized by elevated sodium, potassium, and chloride levels in sweat.

538
01:07:01,674 --> 01:07:06,024
Lung involvement is commonplace and is usually the cause of death.

539
01:07:06,024 --> 01:07:16,287
Bronchial gland hypertrophy with mucous plugging and small-airway obstruction leads to subsequent infection that ultimately causes chronic bronchitis and bronchiectasis.

540
01:07:16,287 --> 01:07:26,274
For complex and not completely explicable reasons, chronic inflammation is caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, S aureus, and H influenzae.

541
01:07:26,274 --> 01:07:36,562
Acute and chronic parenchymal inflammation ultimately causes extensive fibrosis, and along with airway obstruction, ventilation–perfusion mismatch develops.

542
01:07:36,562 --> 01:07:39,987
Pulmonary insufficiency is the end result.

543
01:07:39,987 --> 01:07:49,549
Currently, the median predicted survival is 37 years, and nearly 80 percent of females with cystic fibrosis now survive to adulthood .

544
01:07:49,549 --> 01:08:01,824
As discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 342), phenotypes vary widely, even among homozygotes for the common Phe508del (∆F508) mutation.

545
01:08:01,824 --> 01:08:09,662
Disease severity depends on which two alleles are inherited, and approximately 10 percent are disease-causing mutations .

546
01:08:09,662 --> 01:08:20,174
Homozygosity for ∆F508 is one of the most severe, and 90 percent of individuals with clinical disease carry at least one F508 allele.

547
01:08:20,174 --> 01:08:23,187
# Preconceptional Counseling.

548
01:08:23,187 --> 01:08:29,012
Women with clinical cystic fibrosis are subfertile because of tenacious cervical mucus.

549
01:08:29,012 --> 01:08:36,474
Traxler and colleagues (2019) recommend in-depth preconceptional counseling regarding contraception.

550
01:08:36,474 --> 01:08:46,499
Males have oligospermia or aspermia from associated congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD), and 98 percent are infertile .

551
01:08:46,499 --> 01:08:54,824
Despite this, the North American Cystic Fibrosis Foundation estimated that 4 percent of affected women become pregnant every year .

552
01:08:54,824 --> 01:09:03,149
The endometrium and tubes express some CFTR but are functionally normal, and the ovaries do not express the CFTR gene.

553
01:09:03,149 --> 01:09:09,487
Both intrauterine insemination and in vitro fertilization can be successful for affected women.

554
01:09:09,487 --> 01:09:16,237
Important factors are the long-term prognosis for the parent(s) and mutation transmission to their offspring.

555
01:09:16,237 --> 01:09:30,587
Screening The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020) recommends that carrier screening be offered to all women, affected by cystic fibrosis or not, who are currently pregnant or considering conception .

556
01:09:30,587 --> 01:09:35,874
The CDC also added cystic fibrosis to newborn screening programs .

557
01:09:35,874 --> 01:09:47,449
The CFTR2 website (www.cftr2.org) helps delineate which gene variants have a clear diseasecausing role and which are benign mutations.

558
01:09:47,449 --> 01:09:49,149
#

559
01:09:49,300 --> 01:09:51,187
Prenatal Care.

560
01:09:51,137 --> 01:09:56,025
Pregnancy outcome is inversely related to severity of lung dysfunction.

561
01:09:56,025 --> 01:10:02,412
Advanced chronic lung disease, hypoxia, and frequent infections may prove deleterious.

562
01:10:02,412 --> 01:10:09,350
At least in the past, cor pulmonale was common, but even that does not preclude successful pregnancy .

563
01:10:09,350 --> 01:10:13,962
In some women, pancreatic dysfunction may cause malnutrition.

564
01:10:13,962 --> 01:10:18,300
Approximately 10 percent have pregestational diabetes .

565
01:10:18,300 --> 01:10:26,012
Otherwise normal pregnancyinduced insulin resistance frequently results in gestational diabetes after midpregnancy.

566
01:10:26,012 --> 01:10:33,200
In one study of 48 pregnancies, half had pancreatic insufficiency, and a third required insulin .

567
01:10:33,200 --> 01:10:36,937
Cystic fibrosis is not affected by pregnancy.

568
01:10:36,937 --> 01:10:43,475
Early reports of a deleterious effect on the course of cystic fibrosis were related to severe disease .

569
01:10:43,475 --> 01:10:51,462
When matched with nonpregnant women by disease severity, some reports indicate no deleterious effects on long-term survival .

570
01:10:51,462 --> 01:10:53,687
# Management.

571
01:10:53,687 --> 01:10:56,787
Prepregnancy counseling is imperative.

572
01:10:56,787 --> 01:11:05,137
Women who choose to become pregnant require close surveillance for development of superimposed infection, diabetes, and heart failure.

573
01:11:05,137 --> 01:11:10,850
Serial pulmonary function testing assists management and estimates prognosis.

574
01:11:10,850 --> 01:11:16,750
If the FEV1 is at least 50 percent, women usually tolerate pregnancy well .

575
01:11:16,750 --> 01:11:23,062
Emphasis is placed on postural drainage, bronchodilator therapy, and infection control.

576
01:11:23,062 --> 01:11:27,762
β-Adrenergic bronchodilators help control airway constriction.

577
01:11:27,762 --> 01:11:34,712
Inhaled recombinant human deoxyribonuclease I improves lung function by reducing sputum viscosity .

578
01:11:34,712 --> 01:11:39,400
Inhaled 7-percent saline produces shortand long-term benefits.

579
01:11:39,400 --> 01:11:44,550
Nutritional status is assessed, and appropriate dietary counseling given.

580
01:11:44,550 --> 01:11:49,175
Calcium and vitamin D are given for poor bone mineralization.

581
01:11:49,175 --> 01:11:54,862
Pancreatic insufficiency requires replacement of oral pancreatic enzymes.

582
01:11:54,862 --> 01:12:00,900
Promising new therapy to correct CFTR protein dysfunction has been recently described.

583
01:12:00,900 --> 01:12:15,562
Using a combination of “CFTR correctors” (tezacaftor–ivacaftor congeners), patients homozygous for the Phe508del mutation significantly benefitted (Davies, 2018; Keating, 2018).

584
01:12:15,562 --> 01:12:19,825
No reports of these drugs are available regarding pregnant women.

585
01:12:19,825 --> 01:12:24,212
Infection is heralded by increasing cough and mucus production.

586
01:12:24,212 --> 01:12:31,225
Oral semisynthetic penicillins or cephalosporins usually suffice to treat staphylococcal infections.

587
01:12:31,225 --> 01:12:39,400
Pseudomonas infection is problematic, and inhaled tobramycin and colistin have been used successfully to control this organism.

588
01:12:39,400 --> 01:12:45,637
Serious pulmonary infections are treated with immediate hospitalization and aggressive therapy.

589
01:12:45,637 --> 01:12:50,037
The threshold for hospitalization with other complications is low.

590
01:12:50,037 --> 01:12:54,812
For labor and delivery, epidural analgesia is recommended .

591
01:12:54,812 --> 01:12:57,450
# Pregnancy Outcome.

592
01:12:57,450 --> 01:13:06,487
Earlier reports chronicled the poor maternal and perinatal outcomes in affected women (Cohen, 1980; Kent, 1993).

593
01:13:06,487 --> 01:13:14,300
More recent reports describe better outcomes, but there still are serious complications (Schlüter, 2019).

594
01:13:14,300 --> 01:13:23,137
Disease severity is now quantified by pulmonary function studies, which are the best predictor of pregnancy and long-term maternal outcome.

595
01:13:23,137 --> 01:13:38,087
One report of 69 pregnancies found that if prepregnancy FEV1 was <60 percent of predicted, the risk for preterm delivery, respiratory complications, and death of the mother within a few years of childbirth was substantive .

596
01:13:38,087 --> 01:13:45,250
Others report similar findings (Fitzsimmons, 1996; Thorpe-Beeston, 2013).

597
01:13:45,250 --> 01:13:55,662
Of 75 pregnancies from the French Cystic Fibrosis Registry, almost 20 percent of newborns were delivered preterm, and 30 percent had growth restriction .

598
01:13:55,662 --> 01:14:02,875
The one maternal death was due to Pseudomonas sepsis in a woman whose prepregnancy FEV1 was 60 percent.

599
01:14:02,875 --> 01:14:10,112
Long-term, however, 17 percent of women died, and four infants had confirmed cystic fibrosis.

600
01:14:10,112 --> 01:14:19,675
Meconium peritonitis is found in 10 to 20 percent of affected fetuses (Boczar, 2015; Terlizzi, 2016).

601
01:14:19,675 --> 01:14:28,125
In a more recent report from the French registry, women with pregestational diabetes had outcomes similar to those without diabetes .

602
01:14:28,125 --> 01:14:41,562
Patel and associates (2015) queried the National Inpatient Sample database and reported that the prevalence of cystic fibrosis in pregnancy had a significant linear rise from 2000 to 2010.

603
01:14:41,562 --> 01:14:44,712
They analyzed 1119 affected

604
01:14:44,884 --> 01:14:47,859
women and reported a litany of risks .

605
01:14:47,809 --> 01:14:52,146
In contrast, perinatal outcomes were surprisingly good.

606
01:14:52,146 --> 01:14:54,646
Table 54-8.

607
01:14:54,646 --> 01:15:04,046
Odds Ratios for Maternal Complications in 1119 Pregnant Women with Cystic Fibrosis Compared with Controls # Lung Transplantation.

608
01:15:04,046 --> 01:15:09,559
Cystic fibrosis is a common antecedent disease leading to lung transplantation.

609
01:15:09,559 --> 01:15:18,684
Gyi and coworkers (2006) reviewed 10 pregnancies in women following transplantation and reported nine liveborn neonates.

610
01:15:18,684 --> 01:15:21,671
Maternal outcomes were less favorable.

611
01:15:21,671 --> 01:15:32,509
Of the three gravidas who developed rejection during pregnancy, all had progressively declining pulmonary function and died of chronic rejection by 38 months after delivery.

612
01:15:32,509 --> 01:15:40,021
More recent international data show a median survival of 10.5 years in patients living beyond the first year .

613
01:15:40,021 --> 01:15:42,534
Carbon Monoxide Poisoning.

614
01:15:42,534 --> 01:15:51,546
Carbon monoxide is a ubiquitous gas, and most nonsmoking adults have a carbon monoxyhemoglobin saturation of 1 to 3 percent.

615
01:15:51,546 --> 01:15:58,271
In cigarette smokers, levels may be 5 to 15 percent, and polycythemia is comorbid.

616
01:15:58,271 --> 01:16:02,946
Carbon monoxide is the most frequent cause of poisoning worldwide .

617
01:16:02,946 --> 01:16:09,009
Toxic levels are often encountered in inadequately ventilated areas warmed by space heaters.

618
01:16:09,009 --> 01:16:16,809
Carbon monoxide is particularly toxic because it is odorless and tasteless and has a high affinity for hemoglobin binding.

619
01:16:16,809 --> 01:16:22,471
Thus, it displaces oxygen and impedes its transfer with resultant hypoxia.

620
01:16:22,471 --> 01:16:36,421
Besides acute sequelae including death and anoxic encephalopathy, cognitive defects develop in as many as half of patients following loss of consciousness or in those with carbon monoxide levels >25 percent .

621
01:16:36,421 --> 01:16:47,034
Hypoxic brain damage has a predilection for the cerebral cortex and white matter and for the basal ganglia (Lo, 2007; Prockop, 2007).

622
01:16:47,034 --> 01:16:51,159
A Parkinson syndrome sometimes follows after recovery.

623
01:16:51,159 --> 01:16:54,971
# Pregnancy and Carbon Monoxide Poisoning.

624
01:16:54,971 --> 01:17:03,184
Through several physiological alterations, the rate of endogenous carbon monoxide production almost doubles in normal pregnancy .

625
01:17:03,184 --> 01:17:15,296
Although the pregnant woman is not more susceptible to carbon monoxide poisoning, the fetus does not tolerate excessive exposure (Friedman, 2015; Nowadly, 2018).

626
01:17:15,296 --> 01:17:23,309
With chronic exposure, maternal symptoms usually appear when the carboxyhemoglobin concentration is 5 to 20 percent.

627
01:17:23,309 --> 01:17:31,334
Symptoms include headache, weakness, dizziness, physical and visual impairment, palpitations, and nausea and vomiting.

628
01:17:31,334 --> 01:17:39,196
With acute exposure, concentrations of 30 to 50 percent produce symptoms of impending cardiovascular collapse.

629
01:17:39,196 --> 01:17:43,321
Levels >50 percent may be fatal for the mother.

630
01:17:43,321 --> 01:17:53,271
Because hemoglobin F has an even higher affinity for carbon monoxide, fetal carboxyhemoglobin levels are 10 to 15 percent higher than those in the mother.

631
01:17:53,271 --> 01:17:56,496
This may be due to facilitated diffusion .

632
01:17:56,496 --> 01:18:03,384
Importantly, the half-life of carboxyhemoglobin is 2 hours in the mother but 7 hours in the fetus.

633
01:18:03,384 --> 01:18:13,446
Carbon monoxide is bound so tightly to hemoglobin F, the fetus may be hypoxic even before maternal carbon monoxide levels are appreciably elevated.

634
01:18:13,446 --> 01:18:26,371
Several anomalies are associated with embryonic exposure, and anoxic encephalopathy is the primary sequela of later fetal exposure (Alehan, 2007; Nowadly, 2018).

635
01:18:26,371 --> 01:18:28,446
# Treatment.

636
01:18:28,446 --> 01:18:37,546
For all victims, treatment of carbon monoxide poisoning is supportive along with immediate administration of 100-percent inspired oxygen.

637
01:18:37,546 --> 01:18:43,446
Indications for hyperbaric oxygen treatment in nonpregnant individuals are unclear .

638
01:18:43,446 --> 01:18:55,859
Weaver and associates (2002) reported that hyperbaric oxygen treatment minimized the incidence of cognitive defects in adults at both 6 weeks and 1 year compared with normobaric oxygen.

639
01:18:55,859 --> 01:19:06,234
Hyperbaric oxygen is generally recommended in pregnancy if carbon monoxide exposure has been “significant” (Aubard, 2000; Ernst, 1998).

640
01:19:06,234 --> 01:19:09,884
The problem is how to define significant exposure .

641
01:19:09,884 --> 01:19:21,409
Although maternal carbon monoxide levels are not accurately predictive of those in the fetus, some clinicians recommend hyperbaric therapy if maternal levels exceed 15 to 20 percent.

642
01:19:21,409 --> 01:19:34,046
Towers and Corcoran (2009) described affected fetuses to have an elevated baseline heart rate, diminished variability, and absent accelerations and decelerations with fetal monitoring.

643
01:19:34,046 --> 01:19:35,771
Treatment of

644
01:19:35,932 --> 01:19:40,707
the affected newborn with hyperbaric oxygen also is controversial .

645
01:19:40,657 --> 01:19:48,369
Elkharrat and coworkers (1991) described successful hyperbaric treatments in 44 pregnant women.

646
01:19:48,369 --> 01:19:59,857
In another report, a woman’s abnormal neurological and cardiopulmonary findings abated in a parallel fashion with resolution of associated fetal heart rate variable decelerations .

647
01:19:59,857 --> 01:20:12,532
Greingor and coworkers (2001) used 2.5-atm hyperbaric 100-percent oxygen for 90 minutes in a 21-week-pregnant woman who was delivered of a healthy neonate at term.

648
01:20:12,532 --> 01:20:19,644
The Undersea and Hyperbaric Medical Society (2021) has a list of accredited facilities.

